INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'basic', 'multiple_choice', 'The fundamental structural unit of ALL knitted fabric (weft or warp knit) is the:', '["Interlacing point of two perpendicular yarn systems","Interlooping loop (stitch), formed when one loop is drawn through a previously formed loop","A twisted yarn bundle","A woven float"]'::jsonb, 'Interlooping loop (stitch), formed when one loop is drawn through a previously formed loop', 'Unlike weaving (interlacing two perpendicular yarn systems, warp and weft), ALL knitting — weft or warp — is built from interlooping: each new loop is drawn through the previous loop, and this loop-through-loop structure is what gives knitted fabric its characteristic stretch and different mechanical behavior from woven fabric.', 1, '{"knit-fundamentals"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Knitted fabric generally has significantly more stretch/elasticity than woven fabric of similar yarn and density mainly because:', '["Knit yarn is always made of spandex","The loop structure itself can deform (open up and close) under tension far more than the relatively fixed interlacing geometry of woven fabric, giving inherent stretch even with 100% inelastic yarn","Knitting always uses a coarser yarn than weaving","Knitted fabric has no yarn crimp at all"]'::jsonb, 'The loop structure itself can deform (open up and close) under tension far more than the relatively fixed interlacing geometry of woven fabric, giving inherent stretch even with 100% inelastic yarn', 'Even knit fabric made from completely inelastic yarn (e.g., 100% cotton with no spandex) stretches noticeably, because the loop geometry itself can distort/elongate under tension in a way that woven interlacing cannot — this structural, not fiber-based, elasticity is knitting''s defining mechanical advantage.', 2, '{"knit-vs-woven","stretch"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'basic', 'multiple_choice', '''Courses'' and ''wales'' in knitted fabric terminology correspond to:', '["Courses = the fabric''s horizontal rows of loops (like weft), wales = the fabric''s vertical columns of loops (like warp)","Courses = vertical columns, wales = horizontal rows","Both terms describe the exact same direction","Neither term has any defined meaning in knitting"]'::jsonb, 'Courses = the fabric''s horizontal rows of loops (like weft), wales = the fabric''s vertical columns of loops (like warp)', 'A course is one complete horizontal row of loops formed in one knitting cycle (analogous to a weft pick), while a wale is a vertical column of interlooped stitches running the length of the fabric (analogous to warp direction) — courses-per-inch (CPI) and wales-per-inch (WPI) together define stitch density, similar to how PPI/EPI define woven fabric density.', 1, '{"courses-wales"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Weft knitting and warp knitting are the two fundamental knitting categories. The key structural difference is:', '["Weft knitting uses one continuous yarn forming loops sequentially across a course (can be hand-knitted or run on relatively simple machines); warp knitting uses many yarns simultaneously, each forming loops in its own wale, requiring a beam-fed machine","They are identical processes with different marketing names","Warp knitting can only be done by hand, never by machine","Weft knitting always produces wider fabric than warp knitting"]'::jsonb, 'Weft knitting uses one continuous yarn forming loops sequentially across a course (can be hand-knitted or run on relatively simple machines); warp knitting uses many yarns simultaneously, each forming loops in its own wale, requiring a beam-fed machine', 'Weft knitting (jersey, rib, interlock, etc.) builds a course loop-by-loop from a single yarn feed moving across the fabric width — this is why hand-knitting is possible and why a single dropped stitch can run/ladder. Warp knitting (tricot, raschel) feeds a full beam of individual yarns, each forming its own vertical wale of loops simultaneously — inherently run-resistant and requiring dedicated machinery.', 2, '{"weft-vs-warp-knitting"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A key practical advantage of warp knitting (e.g., tricot) over weft knitting for certain applications is:', '["Warp knit fabric always stretches more than weft knit fabric","Warp knit fabric strongly resists ''laddering/running'' (a dropped stitch propagating down a full wale), since each wale is formed by its own separate yarn rather than a single continuous thread shared across the whole fabric","Warp knitting machines are always cheaper than weft knitting machines","Warp knitting cannot use synthetic filament yarn"]'::jsonb, 'Warp knit fabric strongly resists ''laddering/running'' (a dropped stitch propagating down a full wale), since each wale is formed by its own separate yarn rather than a single continuous thread shared across the whole fabric', 'The classic ''run in stockings'' problem happens because weft-knit fine-gauge fabric uses one continuous yarn per course, so a dropped/broken loop can unravel down the entire wale; warp knitting''s structure (separate yarn per wale, diagonally interlinked) is inherently much more run-resistant, which is why warp knit tricot is favored for run-resistant applications like some lingerie linings and technical textiles.', 3, '{"warp-knitting","run-resistance"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Latch needles'' (used in most weft knitting machines) form loops using a hinged latch that opens and closes automatically as the needle moves. Compared to older ''bearded/spring'' needles, latch needles are preferred mainly because they:', '["Cannot knit any yarn type","Operate independently and reliably at high speed without needing an external closing mechanism (presser), since the latch self-actuates from the loop''s own movement, simplifying high-speed circular/flat knitting machine design","Only work for warp knitting, not weft knitting","Require manual closing after every single stitch by an operator"]'::jsonb, 'Operate independently and reliably at high speed without needing an external closing mechanism (presser), since the latch self-actuates from the loop''s own movement, simplifying high-speed circular/flat knitting machine design', 'Bearded needles require a separate ''presser'' mechanism to close the needle''s hook at the right moment in the knitting cycle, limiting achievable speed and machine complexity; latch needles self-open and self-close as the old loop physically pushes the latch during needle movement, enabling much higher, more reliable automatic operation — the basis of virtually all modern high-speed weft knitting machines.', 3, '{"latch-needle"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Compound needles'' (used in many modern high-speed circular knitting machines, especially fine gauge) differ from latch needles in that they:', '["Use two separate moving parts (a hook part and a sliding closing tongue) rather than a single hinged latch, allowing gentler yarn handling and higher achievable speeds, especially valuable at very fine gauges","Cannot form any loop structure","Are only used in warp knitting, never weft knitting","Eliminate the need for any needle bed"]'::jsonb, 'Use two separate moving parts (a hook part and a sliding closing tongue) rather than a single hinged latch, allowing gentler yarn handling and higher achievable speeds, especially valuable at very fine gauges', 'Compound needles use a hook and a separate sliding tongue that closes the hook via a smooth sliding motion rather than a swinging latch — this reduces the mechanical shock/impact on yarn during loop formation, allowing higher machine speeds and gentler handling of fine or sensitive yarns compared to traditional latch needles.', 3, '{"compound-needle"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', '''Sinkers'' in a weft knitting machine work alongside needles primarily to:', '["Dye the yarn during knitting","Hold down the old loops on the needle bed while new loops are being formed, controlling loop size/depth and helping the fabric feed away smoothly","Cut the yarn between courses","Measure the exact final GSM of the fabric"]'::jsonb, 'Hold down the old loops on the needle bed while new loops are being formed, controlling loop size/depth and helping the fabric feed away smoothly', 'Sinkers are thin metal blades positioned between needles that hold the previously formed loops down against the needle bed while the needle rises to form a new loop — this prevents the old loops from being pulled up with the needle and helps regulate stitch/loop length, directly influencing fabric density and appearance.', 2, '{"sinkers"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Loop length'' (the actual length of yarn used to form each individual stitch) is one of the most important knitting parameters because it directly controls:', '["Only the fabric''s color depth","Fabric GSM, dimensional stability/shrinkage potential, and stretch — shorter loop length generally gives tighter, heavier fabric with less stretch, while longer loop length gives looser, lighter, stretchier fabric","The knitting machine''s brand and origin","Only the yarn''s twist direction"]'::jsonb, 'Fabric GSM, dimensional stability/shrinkage potential, and stretch — shorter loop length generally gives tighter, heavier fabric with less stretch, while longer loop length gives looser, lighter, stretchier fabric', 'Loop length is the single most influential knitting-machine-controllable parameter for fabric properties: it directly sets stitch density and therefore GSM, and it also strongly influences the fabric''s dimensional (shrinkage) behavior and stretch/recovery characteristics — mills tightly control loop length as a primary quality/spec parameter, often more critical than yarn count alone.', 3, '{"loop-length"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'A knitting mill wants to REDUCE a jersey fabric''s GSM (make it lighter) without changing the yarn count. The most direct machine-setting change to achieve this is:', '["Decrease machine gauge (fewer needles per inch)","Increase loop length (looser stitch setting), which reduces the amount of yarn packed into a given fabric area, directly lowering GSM","Increase the knitting machine''s rotational speed","Change the yarn''s twist direction"]'::jsonb, 'Increase loop length (looser stitch setting), which reduces the amount of yarn packed into a given fabric area, directly lowering GSM', 'Since loop length directly sets how much yarn is used per stitch and therefore per unit fabric area, increasing loop length (a looser stitch setting on the machine) is the standard, most direct lever to reduce GSM at a constant yarn count — changing gauge would require a different needle configuration entirely, a far bigger and less flexible change.', 2, '{"loop-length","GSM-control"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Knitting machine ''gauge'' (e.g., 24 gauge, meaning needles per inch of needle bed/cylinder) fundamentally limits:', '["The fabric''s final color only","The maximum yarn count (thickness) that can physically be knitted on that machine, and the finest achievable stitch density — finer gauge (more needles/inch) requires proportionally finer yarn","The machine''s electricity consumption exclusively","Nothing related to yarn selection"]'::jsonb, 'The maximum yarn count (thickness) that can physically be knitted on that machine, and the finest achievable stitch density — finer gauge (more needles/inch) requires proportionally finer yarn', 'Gauge sets the physical spacing between needles, which directly constrains how thick a yarn can pass through the needle spaces and hooks without jamming — a fine-gauge (high needles-per-inch) machine can only run fine, thin yarns, while coarse-gauge machines are needed for thick/bulky yarns, making gauge and yarn count selection tightly interdependent decisions.', 2, '{"knitting-gauge"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Single jersey (plain weft knit) fabric has a well-known tendency to curl at its cut edges. This happens because:', '["The fabric is always finished with a curling chemical","The asymmetric structure of the plain knit loop creates unbalanced internal forces that, when the fabric edge is cut and no longer restrained by the surrounding fabric, cause the edge to roll toward the technical face on the sides and technical back at top/bottom","Single jersey has no loops at all, causing the curling","Curling only happens if the fabric is dyed a dark color"]'::jsonb, 'The asymmetric structure of the plain knit loop creates unbalanced internal forces that, when the fabric edge is cut and no longer restrained by the surrounding fabric, cause the edge to roll toward the technical face on the sides and technical back at top/bottom', 'Plain/single jersey loops are inherently asymmetric (all loops face the same way on the technical face vs. back), storing directional internal stress that''s balanced out within the continuous fabric — cutting releases this restraint at the edge, letting the stored stress curl the free edge; this is a well-known structural characteristic that affects cutting/sewing planning for single jersey garments.', 3, '{"single-jersey","curling"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Rib knit fabric (e.g., 1x1 or 2x2 rib, used for cuffs/collars) achieves its characteristic high widthwise stretch and ''non-curling'' edge property mainly because it:', '["Uses no loops at all, unlike jersey","Alternates knit (face) and purl (back) wales across the fabric width, and this structural alternation balances the internal stresses that cause single jersey to curl, while also giving strong widthwise elastic recovery","Is always made with 100% spandex yarn regardless of the base fiber","Has identical structure to single jersey with a different name"]'::jsonb, 'Alternates knit (face) and purl (back) wales across the fabric width, and this structural alternation balances the internal stresses that cause single jersey to curl, while also giving strong widthwise elastic recovery', 'Rib structure alternates the loop ''direction'' (knit-face wales next to purl-face wales) across the width — this balances the directional stresses that cause single jersey''s curling tendency (so rib lies flat), and the alternating structure also gives rib fabric its notable widthwise stretch and recovery, which is exactly why it''s chosen for cuffs, collars, and waistbands that need to stretch and snap back.', 2, '{"rib-knit"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Interlock fabric is essentially two 1x1 rib structures knitted together, interlocking on both faces. Compared to single jersey of the same yarn, interlock generally offers:', '["Less dimensional stability and a rougher hand-feel","Greater thickness/weight, better dimensional stability, a smooth identical appearance on both faces, and reduced stretch compared to single jersey or even standard rib","Identical stretch and appearance properties to plain jersey","No difference in structure from a basic 1x1 rib"]'::jsonb, 'Greater thickness/weight, better dimensional stability, a smooth identical appearance on both faces, and reduced stretch compared to single jersey or even standard rib', 'Because interlock interlaces two rib structures together into a denser double-faced fabric, it''s inherently thicker, heavier, and more dimensionally stable than single jersey, with a smooth reversible face on both sides — but this same density/interlocking reduces its stretch compared to open single-faced structures, a classic structure-property tradeoff.', 3, '{"interlock-knit"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Purl (links-links) knit structure alternates knit and purl stitches within the SAME wale (unlike rib, which alternates between different wales). This gives purl fabric its distinctive property of:', '["Behaving identically to plain jersey in every direction","Stretching significantly in BOTH the lengthwise (course) and widthwise (wale) directions, unlike jersey which stretches much more in width than length","Zero stretch in any direction, like a woven fabric","Only being producible on warp knitting machines"]'::jsonb, 'Stretching significantly in BOTH the lengthwise (course) and widthwise (wale) directions, unlike jersey which stretches much more in width than length', 'Purl structure''s within-wale alternation of knit/purl loops gives it a unique symmetric appearance (looks the same on both faces, resembling hand-knit garter stitch) and, notably, meaningful stretch in the lengthwise direction as well as widthwise — a property plain jersey and most rib structures don''t share, making purl useful for specific stretch-in-all-directions applications.', 3, '{"purl-knit"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Tuck stitch'' (where a needle holds an old loop while also collecting a new loop, without knocking over, creating a small raised/textured surface effect) differs from a ''float/miss stitch'' in that:', '["They are the exact same technique with different names","Tuck stitch actively accumulates an extra loop on the needle (creating visible surface texture and slightly wider, shorter fabric), while float/miss stitch simply skips forming a loop at that needle for that course, leaving the yarn floating behind on the technical back without adding any extra loop","Float stitch is only possible in warp knitting","Tuck stitch is only possible with elastane yarn"]'::jsonb, 'Tuck stitch actively accumulates an extra loop on the needle (creating visible surface texture and slightly wider, shorter fabric), while float/miss stitch simply skips forming a loop at that needle for that course, leaving the yarn floating behind on the technical back without adding any extra loop', 'These are two distinct pattern-forming techniques in weft knitting: tuck stitch holds and combines loops on the needle (a common technique for pique/waffle-type surface texture, e.g., some polo shirt fabrics), while float/miss stitch simply bypasses loop formation at selected needles, leaving a yarn float on the back — both create design/structural effects but via fundamentally different needle actions.', 3, '{"tuck-stitch","float-stitch"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Pique fabric (common in polo shirts) achieves its characteristic raised, textured, slightly ventilated surface primarily through:', '["A special dye process applied after plain jersey knitting","A combination of knit and tuck stitches in a repeating pattern, which creates a slightly three-dimensional, honeycomb-like surface texture directly from the knitting structure itself","Embroidering a raised texture onto flat jersey fabric after knitting","Weaving, not knitting, is always used for pique fabric"]'::jsonb, 'A combination of knit and tuck stitches in a repeating pattern, which creates a slightly three-dimensional, honeycomb-like surface texture directly from the knitting structure itself', 'Pique''s textured surface is a genuine knit STRUCTURE effect (using patterned tuck stitches combined with regular knit stitches in a repeat), not a post-knitting embellishment — this structural texture is also what gives pique fabric its characteristic slightly stiffer hand and good ventilation compared to smooth plain jersey.', 2, '{"pique-knit"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Dropped stitch'' and ''press-off'' are related but distinct knitting defects. A press-off specifically refers to:', '["A single missed stitch that self-corrects on the next course","A needle failing to properly hold/knock over its loop during knitting, causing that needle''s entire wale of loops to be released and unravel/collapse, often creating a visible hole or run in the fabric","A defect only found in woven fabric, not knit","The intentional removal of the fabric from the machine at the end of production"]'::jsonb, 'A needle failing to properly hold/knock over its loop during knitting, causing that needle''s entire wale of loops to be released and unravel/collapse, often creating a visible hole or run in the fabric', 'Press-off is a more severe fault than a simple isolated skipped stitch — it happens when a needle malfunctions (often from a bent hook, broken latch, or yarn tension issue) and fails to properly retain/knock over its loop, causing that needle''s entire column of loops to release and potentially unravel down the fabric, similar in effect to a ladder/run.', 3, '{"press-off","defects"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Needle line'' defect (a persistent, faint vertical line running the length of knit fabric) is typically caused by:', '["A random, non-repeating dye variation","One specific needle in the machine consistently forming a slightly different-sized loop than its neighbors (e.g., from wear, bending, or incorrect setting), creating a visible line repeating at that exact wale position throughout the entire fabric roll","The knitting machine''s rotational speed being too high across all needles equally","The yarn''s twist direction changing mid-course"]'::jsonb, 'One specific needle in the machine consistently forming a slightly different-sized loop than its neighbors (e.g., from wear, bending, or incorrect setting), creating a visible line repeating at that exact wale position throughout the entire fabric roll', 'Because a circular/flat knitting machine has a fixed needle at each wale position, a single defective needle (worn, bent, or improperly gauged) will consistently produce a slightly different loop at that same wale position every single course — this creates a persistent, position-repeating vertical line defect, directly traceable to inspecting/replacing that specific needle.', 3, '{"needle-line","defects"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Barre'' (or barré) defect in knit fabric — faint, repeating horizontal bands/streaks across the width — is most commonly caused by:', '["A single defective needle in the machine","Slight yarn property variations (count, twist, luster, or dye-lot differences) between different yarn feeders/cones being knitted together course by course, becoming visible as horizontal banding once the fabric is dyed or viewed under certain light","The fabric being cut at an angle after knitting","A problem exclusive to woven fabric, not knitting"]'::jsonb, 'Slight yarn property variations (count, twist, luster, or dye-lot differences) between different yarn feeders/cones being knitted together course by course, becoming visible as horizontal banding once the fabric is dyed or viewed under certain light', 'Because a circular knitting machine draws from many simultaneous yarn feeders forming different courses, even small property differences between individual cones/lots (slightly different count, twist, dye uptake) show up as periodic horizontal banding across the fabric — a persistent challenge in knit quality control, often only becoming clearly visible after dyeing under strong or reflected light.', 3, '{"barre-defect"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A knit fabric mill sources yarn for a single fabric lot from 20 different supplier cones/lots rather than requiring a single uniform source. To minimize barre defect risk, the most important control measure is:', '["No control is needed, since barre defect is unrelated to yarn sourcing","Testing and matching key yarn properties (count, dye affinity/lot, twist) across all cones before knitting, and/or physically randomizing/distributing different-lot cones across the machine''s feeders rather than grouping them, so any small variation is spread out rather than concentrated","Using the cheapest available yarn regardless of consistency","Increasing the knitting machine''s speed to reduce total knitting time"]'::jsonb, 'Testing and matching key yarn properties (count, dye affinity/lot, twist) across all cones before knitting, and/or physically randomizing/distributing different-lot cones across the machine''s feeders rather than grouping them, so any small variation is spread out rather than concentrated', 'Since barre arises from feeder-to-feeder yarn property differences, mills reduce risk by both verifying yarn consistency before knitting AND by strategically distributing cones from different lots across the machine''s many feeders (rather than clustering same-lot cones together), which spreads any residual variation more evenly and makes it far less visually noticeable as a banding pattern.', 3, '{"barre-defect","quality-control"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Circular knitting machines are specified partly by their cylinder diameter (e.g., 30-inch diameter). This diameter primarily determines:', '["The fabric''s final color","The maximum tubular fabric width/circumference achievable on that machine, which relates to what garment sizes/panel widths can be efficiently cut from the resulting fabric tube","The yarn''s twist direction requirement","The knitting machine''s electricity cost only"]'::jsonb, 'The maximum tubular fabric width/circumference achievable on that machine, which relates to what garment sizes/panel widths can be efficiently cut from the resulting fabric tube', 'Circular knitting produces a continuous tube of fabric whose circumference is set by the cylinder''s diameter (and gauge/stitch density); mills select cylinder diameter based on the target garment size range and how efficiently garment panels can be cut from the resulting tube width, balancing fabric utilization against machine cost/availability.', 2, '{"circular-knitting","cylinder-diameter"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Flat knitting machines (V-bed) are generally chosen over circular knitting machines specifically when a garment requires:', '["The absolute highest possible production speed for plain, unshaped fabric","''Fully-fashioned'' shaping — increasing/decreasing the number of active needles mid-knitting to directly knit a garment panel''s shape (e.g., a sweater''s armhole or shoulder taper) without needing to cut the shape from flat fabric afterward","Only tubular, seamless fabric with no shaping capability","A fabric with no possible pattern or texture variation"]'::jsonb, '''Fully-fashioned'' shaping — increasing/decreasing the number of active needles mid-knitting to directly knit a garment panel''s shape (e.g., a sweater''s armhole or shoulder taper) without needing to cut the shape from flat fabric afterward', 'V-bed flat knitting machines can selectively activate/deactivate individual needles across the bed width during knitting, allowing the fabric panel''s width to be increased or decreased mid-production to directly knit a shaped garment piece (fully-fashioned) — circular machines, producing a continuous uniform-width tube, cannot do this in-process shaping, which is why flat knitting dominates shaped knitwear like fine-gauge sweaters.', 3, '{"flat-knitting","fully-fashioned"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', '''Seamless'' knitting technology (producing a complete, ready-shaped garment like a sock or bodywear piece directly on the machine, with minimal or no cutting/sewing) offers which main production advantage?', '["It always uses more fabric and creates more waste than cut-and-sew methods","It significantly reduces fabric waste (no cutting scraps) and labor (minimal sewing), while also eliminating side seams that could otherwise cause chafing/discomfort in tight-fitting garments like socks, hosiery, or activewear","It cannot produce any garment with shaping or fit variation","It only works for woven, not knit, fabric"]'::jsonb, 'It significantly reduces fabric waste (no cutting scraps) and labor (minimal sewing), while also eliminating side seams that could otherwise cause chafing/discomfort in tight-fitting garments like socks, hosiery, or activewear', 'Seamless knitting machines (a specialized category of circular knitting with programmable shaping) knit a garment to its near-final 3D shape directly, drastically cutting fabric waste from cutting and labor from sewing, while also removing side seams — a real functional benefit for tight, performance-oriented garments where seam chafing/pressure points are a genuine comfort and performance issue.', 2, '{"seamless-knitting"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Plating'' technique in knitting (feeding two yarns simultaneously into the same needle so one yarn consistently appears on the technical face and the other on the technical back) is commonly used to:', '["Increase the knitting machine''s speed with no effect on fabric appearance","Combine a cheaper/functional yarn (e.g., cotton for comfort/absorbency) on the inner face against skin with a different yarn (e.g., polyester for moisture-wicking, or a color-contrast yarn) on the outer face, giving two-sided functional or aesthetic properties from a single knitting pass","Remove all yarn twist before knitting","Only work with 100% identical yarns on both feeds"]'::jsonb, 'Combine a cheaper/functional yarn (e.g., cotton for comfort/absorbency) on the inner face against skin with a different yarn (e.g., polyester for moisture-wicking, or a color-contrast yarn) on the outer face, giving two-sided functional or aesthetic properties from a single knitting pass', 'Plating deliberately controls which of two simultaneously-fed yarns dominates each face of the loop, letting mills engineer fabrics with different functional or visual properties on the inside versus outside (e.g., moisture-wicking synthetic facing outward, soft cotton facing skin, or a color-contrast reversible effect) — all achieved in a single knitting pass rather than requiring a separate lamination or coating step.', 3, '{"plating"}'::text[]);
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Adding spandex/elastane (as a bare filament or covered yarn) into a cotton knit structure (e.g., for stretch t-shirts) primarily improves the fabric''s:', '["Color depth only","Elastic recovery (the fabric''s ability to stretch and then snap back to its original shape), reducing the baggy, permanently-stretched-out look that pure cotton knits can develop with wear","Only the fabric''s weight, with no effect on stretch behavior","Breathability, with no relationship to stretch"]'::jsonb, 'Elastic recovery (the fabric''s ability to stretch and then snap back to its original shape), reducing the baggy, permanently-stretched-out look that pure cotton knits can develop with wear', 'While cotton knit already has structural stretch from its loop geometry, it has poor RECOVERY (elastic snap-back) — after repeated stretching (sitting, moving), pure cotton knit garments tend to bag out permanently at knees/elbows; adding spandex specifically adds genuine elastic recovery, keeping the garment''s fit closer to original shape over repeated wear.', 2, '{"spandex","elastic-recovery"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Bursting strength testing (rather than a woven-style tensile/tear strength test) is the standard mechanical strength test specifically used for knitted fabric because:', '["Bursting strength has no relevance to knit fabric performance","Knit fabric''s loop structure means it deforms/distorts multi-directionally under stress rather than failing along a single straight line like woven fabric under directional pull, so a multi-directional burst (ball-shaped probe pushed through the fabric) better reflects real-world knit fabric failure behavior","Bursting strength is only used for paper products, not textiles","Woven fabric cannot be tensile tested at all, unlike knit"]'::jsonb, 'Knit fabric''s loop structure means it deforms/distorts multi-directionally under stress rather than failing along a single straight line like woven fabric under directional pull, so a multi-directional burst (ball-shaped probe pushed through the fabric) better reflects real-world knit fabric failure behavior', 'Because knit fabric''s interlooped structure allows it to stretch and redistribute stress in multiple directions simultaneously (unlike the more directionally-fixed woven interlacing), a simple single-direction tensile pull test doesn''t represent how knit fabric actually fails in use — bursting strength (a probe pushed perpendicular through a clamped circular fabric sample) captures this multi-directional failure mode more realistically.', 3, '{"bursting-strength","testing"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Knit fabric ''spirality'' (a defect where the fabric''s wales twist diagonally rather than running perfectly vertically, causing garment side seams to twist after washing) is most commonly caused by:', '["The fabric being knitted at too low a temperature","Residual torque/twist liveliness in the yarn itself (especially high-twist singles yarn) that wasn''t fully balanced/set before knitting, which the knit structure then reveals as a systematic diagonal distortion, often worsening after relaxation/washing","Using too wide a knitting machine cylinder","Incorrect fabric folding during storage only, unrelated to yarn or knitting"]'::jsonb, 'Residual torque/twist liveliness in the yarn itself (especially high-twist singles yarn) that wasn''t fully balanced/set before knitting, which the knit structure then reveals as a systematic diagonal distortion, often worsening after relaxation/washing', 'Singles yarn (especially with high twist) retains internal torque (''twist liveliness'') from spinning; when knitted, this residual torque causes the loops to systematically skew in one diagonal direction rather than sitting perfectly vertical — the distortion often becomes more visible after the fabric relaxes in washing, making spirality a persistent quality challenge traced back to yarn twist balance (or lack of it) rather than the knitting process itself.', 3, '{"spirality","defects"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A knit garment manufacturer notices spirality complaints specifically on garments made from single jersey using high-twist ring-spun singles yarn, but NOT on garments from the same fabric structure using low-twist or plied yarn. This comparison supports which conclusion?', '["Spirality is unrelated to yarn twist and must have another cause entirely","The evidence directly supports yarn twist liveliness as the root cause, since plied yarn (twist-balanced by the opposing ply twist) and low-twist singles both show less residual torque available to cause the diagonal distortion","Single jersey structure itself is incapable of showing spirality regardless of yarn","The knitting machine must be different between the two cases"]'::jsonb, 'The evidence directly supports yarn twist liveliness as the root cause, since plied yarn (twist-balanced by the opposing ply twist) and low-twist singles both show less residual torque available to cause the diagonal distortion', 'This is a controlled comparison (same fabric structure, different yarn twist characteristics) that isolates yarn twist as the variable — since plied yarn is twist-balanced (opposing ply twist cancels out residual torque) and low-twist singles simply have less torque to begin with, both showing reduced spirality directly confirms yarn twist liveliness, not the knitting process or structure, as the practical root cause in this scenario.', 3, '{"spirality","root-cause"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Loop length variation WITHIN a single knitted fabric roll (not machine-to-machine, but course-to-course inconsistency) primarily causes:', '["No measurable quality issue at all","Uneven GSM and dimensional inconsistency along the fabric roll''s length, potentially causing shade/appearance variation after dyeing (since tighter areas can dye differently than looser areas) and inconsistent shrinkage behavior","Only a color change, unrelated to weight or dimensions","An automatic improvement in fabric strength"]'::jsonb, 'Uneven GSM and dimensional inconsistency along the fabric roll''s length, potentially causing shade/appearance variation after dyeing (since tighter areas can dye differently than looser areas) and inconsistent shrinkage behavior', 'Since loop length is the primary determinant of fabric density/GSM, any inconsistency in loop length feeding (from unstable yarn tension, feeder issues, or machine settings drifting) directly shows up as GSM variation along the roll, which can also cause differential dye uptake (denser areas often appear as a slightly different shade) and inconsistent shrinkage — making loop length consistency a tightly monitored knitting QC parameter.', 3, '{"loop-length","quality-control"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Take-down'' mechanism (the system that pulls the newly formed fabric away from the knitting zone under controlled tension) must be carefully calibrated because too much take-down tension will:', '["Have no effect on the fabric at all","Stretch loops excessively during formation, distorting stitch shape/size, potentially causing needle damage or yarn breakage, and altering the fabric''s actual loop length/GSM from the intended machine setting","Automatically improve the fabric''s final quality with no downside","Only affect the fabric''s color, not its structure"]'::jsonb, 'Stretch loops excessively during formation, distorting stitch shape/size, potentially causing needle damage or yarn breakage, and altering the fabric''s actual loop length/GSM from the intended machine setting', 'Take-down tension must be balanced: too little, and newly formed loops aren''t cleared properly from the needles (risking loop pile-up or knitting faults); too much, and the excess pulling force stretches/distorts the loops as they''re formed, effectively changing the realized loop length (and therefore GSM/dimensions) away from the machine''s nominal setting, while also raising yarn breakage risk.', 3, '{"take-down","tension-control"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Jacquard knitting (creating multi-color or complex patterned designs by selectively knitting or tucking different yarns at individually controlled needles) fundamentally requires which machine capability?', '["No special capability — any basic knitting machine can do this automatically","Individual needle selection control (electronic or mechanical patterning device) so that each needle can independently choose which color yarn/action to perform at each course, similar in principle to how jacquard weaving controls individual warp ends","Only hand-knitting can achieve jacquard patterns","The complete removal of all sinkers from the machine"]'::jsonb, 'Individual needle selection control (electronic or mechanical patterning device) so that each needle can independently choose which color yarn/action to perform at each course, similar in principle to how jacquard weaving controls individual warp ends', 'Just as jacquard weaving requires individual warp-end control for intricate patterns, jacquard knitting requires an individual needle-selection mechanism (historically mechanical, now typically electronic) so each needle can be independently commanded to knit a specific color or perform a specific stitch action at each course — enabling complex multi-color knit patterns beyond simple stripe or structural designs.', 2, '{"jacquard-knitting"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A knit fabric technologist needs to produce a thick, warm, brushed-back fleece fabric. Which combination of knitting and finishing choices is most appropriate?', '["A tight, low-loop-length single jersey with no finishing","A structure incorporating a separate ''fleece'' yarn (often on the technical back, sometimes via a sinker-loop or terry-style knit-in loop) that is subsequently raised/brushed in finishing to create a soft, insulating pile surface","Only warp knitting can ever produce fleece fabric","Fleece fabric requires no special knit structure, only a heavier dye"]'::jsonb, 'A structure incorporating a separate ''fleece'' yarn (often on the technical back, sometimes via a sinker-loop or terry-style knit-in loop) that is subsequently raised/brushed in finishing to create a soft, insulating pile surface', 'Knit fleece fabric typically uses a structure (often a plated or terry-style knit with a specific fleece/backing yarn forming extra loops or sinker loops on the technical back) specifically designed to be raised/brushed during finishing — the mechanical brushing pulls up fiber ends from this backing yarn to form the characteristic soft, insulating pile, similar in principle to woven raising/napping but built on a knit base.', 3, '{"fleece-knit"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Comparing a 20-gauge and a 28-gauge circular knitting machine (both knitting cotton, same yarn count target), the 28-gauge machine will generally produce fabric that is:', '["Coarser and heavier per unit area, with visibly larger loops","Finer, smoother, and with a tighter stitch structure per unit area, since more needles per inch means more, smaller loops packed into the same fabric width","Identical to the 20-gauge fabric in every respect","Only usable for warp knitting, not weft knitting"]'::jsonb, 'Finer, smoother, and with a tighter stitch structure per unit area, since more needles per inch means more, smaller loops packed into the same fabric width', 'Higher gauge numbers mean more needles (and thus more, smaller stitches) per inch of needle bed/cylinder — at a comparable yarn count, this produces a visually finer, smoother fabric surface with a tighter stitch structure, whereas the lower-gauge (20) machine produces a coarser, more open-looking fabric with fewer, larger stitches per inch.', 2, '{"gauge-comparison"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'A garment technologist evaluating fabric for a fitted, seamless-look sportswear application should generally prioritize which knit fabric property alongside GSM and fiber content?', '["Only the fabric''s color fastness rating, with no consideration of stretch","Stretch percentage and elastic recovery% (how fully the fabric snaps back after stretching), since a fitted performance garment depends heavily on the fabric maintaining shape and compression through repeated movement","The fabric''s flame retardant rating exclusively","Only the yarn count, since gauge and structure are irrelevant to fit"]'::jsonb, 'Stretch percentage and elastic recovery% (how fully the fabric snaps back after stretching), since a fitted performance garment depends heavily on the fabric maintaining shape and compression through repeated movement', 'For fitted/compression sportswear, stretch (how much the fabric elongates) alone isn''t sufficient — RECOVERY (how completely it returns to original dimensions after each stretch cycle) is equally or more critical, since poor recovery leads to progressive baggy distortion during actual wear and exercise, directly undermining the fitted performance the garment is designed for.', 2, '{"stretch-recovery","performance-knit"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A knitting mill is deciding between single jersey and interlock structure for a t-shirt fabric where the customer specifically wants minimal risk of visible see-through (opacity) at a given GSM target. The better structural choice is generally:', '["Single jersey, since it is always more opaque than any double-knit structure","Interlock (or another double-knit structure), since its denser, double-faced construction generally gives better opacity/cover than a single-faced structure at a comparable or even lower GSM","Opacity is entirely unrelated to knit structure and depends only on dye color","Neither structure has any measurable difference in opacity"]'::jsonb, 'Interlock (or another double-knit structure), since its denser, double-faced construction generally gives better opacity/cover than a single-faced structure at a comparable or even lower GSM', 'Interlock''s double-layer interlocking construction inherently provides more yarn coverage/density through the fabric''s thickness than a single-faced jersey structure, giving better opacity (less see-through) at a comparable or even somewhat lower GSM — a key reason double-knit structures are chosen when opacity is a specific customer requirement, independent of dye color.', 3, '{"opacity","structure-selection"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Why can single jersey knit fabric shrink noticeably MORE in the length (course) direction than a comparable woven fabric of similar fiber content, if not properly relaxed/finished before garment construction?', '["Knit fabric never shrinks under any circumstances","The knit loop structure holds significant latent tension/distortion from the knitting and take-down process, and relaxation (allowing loops to return toward their natural, un-stretched geometry) causes more dramatic dimensional change than the comparatively fixed woven interlacing geometry","Woven fabric always shrinks more than knit fabric in every case","Shrinkage is entirely determined by fiber type alone, with no structural contribution"]'::jsonb, 'The knit loop structure holds significant latent tension/distortion from the knitting and take-down process, and relaxation (allowing loops to return toward their natural, un-stretched geometry) causes more dramatic dimensional change than the comparatively fixed woven interlacing geometry', 'Because knit loops are pulled into a somewhat stretched/distorted shape during knitting and take-down (under tension), the fabric holds latent ''relaxation shrinkage'' potential — once this tension is released (e.g., in the first wash, or via proper compacting/relaxing finishing), the loops contract toward their natural resting geometry, causing measurably more length-direction shrinkage than a woven fabric''s comparatively fixed interlacing structure typically shows, which is why knit fabric compacting/pre-shrinking is a critical finishing step.', 3, '{"knit-shrinkage"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', '''Compacting'' (a knit-fabric-specific finishing process, mechanically pre-shrinking the fabric in the course direction using a felt calendar or similar mechanism) exists specifically to:', '["Add color to the fabric","Pre-relax/pre-shrink the fabric''s latent knit tension mechanically before garment construction, so the finished garment shrinks minimally (within spec) during the customer''s actual washing","Increase the fabric''s stretch percentage beyond its natural capability","Replace the need for any dyeing process"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-relax/pre-shrink the fabric''s latent knit tension mechanically before garment construction, so the finished garment shrinks minimally (within spec) during the customer''s actual washing', 'Compacting is the knit-fabric equivalent of sanforizing for wovens — it mechanically pre-shrinks the fabric (typically in the length/course direction, where knit shrinkage is often most pronounced) before cutting and sewing, so the finished garment''s later shrinkage during customer washing stays within an acceptable, specified limit.', 2, '{"compacting","knit-finishing"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A quality manager finds that garments cut from the SAME fabric roll, but from panels cut at different points along the roll''s length, show measurably different shrinkage% after wash-testing. Assuming compacting was applied uniformly, what in-process (knitting) factor should be investigated first?', '["The dye recipe used, since it''s unrelated to knitting","Loop length consistency along the roll''s length, since even properly-compacted fabric will retain the underlying dimensional differences from any loop length variation that occurred during knitting","The customer''s washing machine brand","The style of garment being cut, unrelated to the fabric itself"]'::jsonb, 'Loop length consistency along the roll''s length, since even properly-compacted fabric will retain the underlying dimensional differences from any loop length variation that occurred during knitting', 'Compacting reduces OVERALL shrinkage potential but doesn''t eliminate underlying structural variation baked in from inconsistent loop length during knitting — if different sections of the roll were knitted with different loop lengths (density), those sections will retain measurably different shrinkage behavior even after uniform compacting, pointing investigation back to knitting-stage loop length consistency rather than the finishing or garment stage.', 3, '{"shrinkage","root-cause"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Tricot warp knitting (a common warp knit structure, widely used for lingerie linings, swimwear, and technical fabrics) is generally characterized by:', '["Poor dimensional stability and heavy run/ladder risk, similar to weft knit jersey","Good dimensional stability, run resistance, and a smooth, often slightly diagonal-appearing surface structure, well suited to lightweight technical and intimate apparel fabrics","Being producible only from natural fibers, never filament synthetics","Zero stretch in any direction whatsoever"]'::jsonb, 'Good dimensional stability, run resistance, and a smooth, often slightly diagonal-appearing surface structure, well suited to lightweight technical and intimate apparel fabrics', 'Tricot''s warp-knit structure (each wale from its own separately-fed yarn, diagonally interlinked with neighbors) gives it notably better dimensional stability and run resistance than weft knit jersey, along with a characteristically smooth surface — properties that make it the standard choice for lightweight technical fabrics like swimwear linings, lingerie, and some sportswear applications.', 3, '{"tricot","warp-knit-properties"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Raschel warp knitting machines (another major warp knitting category, distinct from tricot) are particularly well suited to producing:', '["Only extremely fine, sheer lingerie fabric with no design variation","A very wide range of fabric types including laces, nets, and heavier technical fabrics, due to their capability for more open, patterned structures and thicker yarn compatibility than typical tricot machines","Only fabrics with zero pattern or structural variation","Fabrics that cannot use any synthetic yarn"]'::jsonb, 'A very wide range of fabric types including laces, nets, and heavier technical fabrics, due to their capability for more open, patterned structures and thicker yarn compatibility than typical tricot machines', 'Raschel machines, with their guide bar and patterning flexibility, are used across an unusually broad range of end products — from delicate laces and open-structure nets to heavier, technical fabrics (e.g., some geotextiles and industrial nets) — distinguishing them from tricot machines, which are more typically associated with finer, smoother apparel-lining-type fabrics.', 3, '{"raschel-knitting"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'A knitting production planner needs to decide machine allocation between a large plain single-jersey t-shirt order and a smaller, complex jacquard-patterned sweater order. The jacquard order will generally require:', '["Faster production speed than the plain jersey order","Slower production speed (due to the individual needle-selection patterning mechanism operating each course) and typically higher per-unit cost, but enables a design complexity the plain jersey machine cannot achieve","Identical machine speed and cost to the plain single jersey order","No machine time at all, since jacquard is applied after knitting"]'::jsonb, 'Slower production speed (due to the individual needle-selection patterning mechanism operating each course) and typically higher per-unit cost, but enables a design complexity the plain jersey machine cannot achieve', 'Individual needle-selection patterning (mechanical or electronic) for jacquard designs inherently adds processing overhead per course compared to a simple, uniform plain jersey knit action — this generally makes jacquard knitting slower and more costly per unit of fabric, a direct tradeoff for the design complexity it enables, which production planners must factor into scheduling and costing.', 2, '{"jacquard-knitting","production-planning"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Run-resistant'' (ladder-resistant) fine-gauge circular knitting for products like hosiery sometimes uses specific structural techniques (e.g., a modified plain structure with periodic locking points) specifically to compensate for weft knitting''s inherent run vulnerability. This illustrates:', '["Weft knitting can never be made run-resistant under any circumstances, making the premise false","Structural/technique modifications can meaningfully improve a knit''s resistance to running even within the weft-knit category, without switching to warp knitting entirely — though typically not matching true warp-knit run resistance","All weft-knit fabrics are automatically completely run-proof by default","Run resistance is entirely unrelated to knit structure and depends only on yarn strength"]'::jsonb, 'Structural/technique modifications can meaningfully improve a knit''s resistance to running even within the weft-knit category, without switching to warp knitting entirely — though typically not matching true warp-knit run resistance', 'While true run immunity is a structural property of warp knitting (separate yarn per wale), certain weft-knit structural techniques and finer/specialized loop-locking approaches can meaningfully reduce (though not eliminate) run propensity within products like fine-gauge hosiery — illustrating that run resistance exists on a spectrum influenced by both broad knitting category and specific structural choices within that category.', 3, '{"run-resistance","hosiery"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A quality control lab measures a knit fabric''s ''stitch density'' (total loops per unit area, i.e., CPI × WPI). Two fabrics have identical stitch density but different loop length settings achieved via different yarn counts. What does this tell a technologist?', '["The two fabrics must be structurally and dimensionally identical in every way","Stitch density alone is insufficient to fully characterize a knit fabric — GSM, hand-feel, and cover can still differ meaningfully between the two, since yarn count (thickness) independently affects how much fabric mass/coverage results from the same loop count","Stitch density has no relationship to any fabric property","Only loop length matters; stitch density is a redundant, meaningless metric"]'::jsonb, 'Stitch density alone is insufficient to fully characterize a knit fabric — GSM, hand-feel, and cover can still differ meaningfully between the two, since yarn count (thickness) independently affects how much fabric mass/coverage results from the same loop count', 'Stitch density (loops per area) and yarn count are independent variables — two fabrics can have the identical number of loops per square inch but differ substantially in GSM, opacity, and hand-feel if one uses much thicker or thinner yarn, which is why full knit fabric characterization requires reporting stitch density, loop length, AND yarn count together, not any single metric alone.', 3, '{"stitch-density","fabric-characterization"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Positive feed'' devices (which deliver yarn to each knitting feeder at a precisely controlled, constant rate rather than letting the needles pull yarn on demand) are used mainly to:', '["Add color patterns to the fabric","Ensure every feeder delivers yarn under consistent, controlled tension regardless of the yarn package''s fill level or machine speed fluctuations, which is critical for maintaining uniform loop length (and therefore GSM/dimensions) across the whole fabric","Replace the need for any needle in the machine","Only matter for warp knitting, not circular weft knitting"]'::jsonb, 'Ensure every feeder delivers yarn under consistent, controlled tension regardless of the yarn package''s fill level or machine speed fluctuations, which is critical for maintaining uniform loop length (and therefore GSM/dimensions) across the whole fabric', 'Without positive feed, yarn is simply pulled off the package by needle demand, and tension can vary as the package empties (changing unwind dynamics) or as machine speed fluctuates — positive feed devices meter yarn out at a precise, constant rate independent of these variables, which is essential for the tight loop-length consistency modern knit quality standards require.', 3, '{"positive-feed"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'A circular knitting machine with MORE feeders (yarn feed points around the cylinder) at the same RPM will generally:', '["Produce fabric at a slower rate than a machine with fewer feeders","Produce fabric at a faster rate, since each full rotation of the cylinder forms one course at every single feeder simultaneously, so more feeders means more courses knitted per revolution","Have no relationship between feeder count and production rate","Only be able to knit a single color regardless of feeder count"]'::jsonb, 'Produce fabric at a faster rate, since each full rotation of the cylinder forms one course at every single feeder simultaneously, so more feeders means more courses knitted per revolution', 'Each feeder around a circular machine''s circumference independently forms a course as the cylinder rotates past it — so a machine with, say, 90 feeders forms 90 courses per single cylinder revolution, versus 48 courses for a 48-feeder machine at the identical RPM, directly multiplying production rate by feeder count.', 2, '{"feeders","production-rate"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Cam systems'' in a circular weft knitting machine are responsible for:', '["Dyeing the yarn as it feeds into the machine","Mechanically guiding the vertical up-down movement path of each needle as it travels around the cylinder, determining whether that needle knits, tucks, or misses at each feeder position","Measuring the finished fabric''s exact GSM","Controlling only the take-down tension, unrelated to needle movement"]'::jsonb, 'Mechanically guiding the vertical up-down movement path of each needle as it travels around the cylinder, determining whether that needle knits, tucks, or misses at each feeder position', 'Cams are precisely shaped tracks that needle butts ride along as the cylinder rotates past each feeder — the cam''s shape at that point determines whether the needle rises fully (knit), partially (tuck), or not at all (miss/float) at that specific feeder, making the cam system the core mechanical logic that determines the knitted structure.', 3, '{"cam-system"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Modern electronic (Jacquard-capable) circular knitting machines replace fixed mechanical cams for needle selection with individually electronically controlled needle selectors. The main advantage over purely mechanical cam-based patterning is:', '["Electronic selection is always slower than mechanical cams","Electronic systems allow rapid, flexible pattern/design changes (via software) without physically changing mechanical cam parts, and support far more complex, larger, or non-repeating patterns than a fixed mechanical cam arrangement practically allows","Electronic machines cannot produce any patterned fabric at all","There is no meaningful difference between the two approaches"]'::jsonb, 'Electronic systems allow rapid, flexible pattern/design changes (via software) without physically changing mechanical cam parts, and support far more complex, larger, or non-repeating patterns than a fixed mechanical cam arrangement practically allows', 'Mechanical cam-based patterning is limited by the physical cam parts installed and typically restricted to smaller, simpler repeats; individually electronically controlled needle selectors (driven by software/design files) can be reprogrammed instantly for new designs and support much larger, more complex, or fully custom patterns — a major flexibility and design-capability advantage for fashion-responsive production.', 3, '{"electronic-knitting","jacquard"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Elastane (spandex) can be incorporated into knit fabric either as ''bare'' elastane (fed directly) or as ''covered'' elastane (wrapped/twisted with another yarn, e.g., nylon-covered spandex). Covered elastane is often preferred in some applications mainly because it:', '["Cannot provide any stretch at all, unlike bare elastane","Offers better protection of the sensitive elastane filament from processing damage (heat, chemicals, abrasion) and easier, more consistent handling on knitting machines, though often at higher cost than bare elastane","Is always cheaper than bare elastane in every case","Eliminates the need for any base yarn in the fabric"]'::jsonb, 'Offers better protection of the sensitive elastane filament from processing damage (heat, chemicals, abrasion) and easier, more consistent handling on knitting machines, though often at higher cost than bare elastane', 'Bare elastane filament is sensitive to heat, certain chemicals, and abrasion during processing; wrapping/covering it with a more robust yarn (like nylon) protects it through knitting, dyeing, and finishing, and can also make it easier to feed consistently on knitting machines — a real cost-vs-protection/handling tradeoff mills weigh based on the specific application and process conditions.', 3, '{"elastane","bare-vs-covered"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Draw ratio (the percentage stretch applied to elastane as it''s fed into the knitting machine, relative to its relaxed length) directly controls:', '["The fabric''s final color depth","How much stretch and recovery power the finished fabric will have, and also affects the fabric''s final dimensions/GSM, since higher draw ratio means the elastane pulls the surrounding fabric structure tighter when it relaxes after knitting","Only the yarn''s twist direction","Nothing measurable about the finished fabric"]'::jsonb, 'How much stretch and recovery power the finished fabric will have, and also affects the fabric''s final dimensions/GSM, since higher draw ratio means the elastane pulls the surrounding fabric structure tighter when it relaxes after knitting', 'Elastane is deliberately fed into the knitting zone under tension (stretched beyond its relaxed length by the draw ratio%); once knitted in and the tension is released, the elastane contracts back toward its relaxed state, pulling the surrounding loop structure with it — higher draw ratio means more contraction force and more resulting fabric stretch/power, but also more dimensional change (fabric pulls in tighter), making draw ratio a critical, carefully specified knitting parameter for stretch fabrics.', 3, '{"elastane","draw-ratio"}'::text[]);
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'French terry knit fabric (popular for sweatshirts) is structurally characterized by having:', '["Two identical smooth jersey faces with no loops on either side","A smooth technical face (like jersey) combined with visible, uncut loops on the technical back, giving softness/warmth on the inside while maintaining a clean flat outer appearance — distinct from fleece, which is typically brushed after knitting","No stretch whatsoever in any direction","A structure that can only be produced by warp knitting"]'::jsonb, 'A smooth technical face (like jersey) combined with visible, uncut loops on the technical back, giving softness/warmth on the inside while maintaining a clean flat outer appearance — distinct from fleece, which is typically brushed after knitting', 'French terry''s defining structural feature is its two-yarn-system construction producing a smooth face and a looped (not brushed/raised) back — the back loops stay as-knitted (uncut, unbrushed), which is the key structural distinction from fleece, where similar backing yarn IS subsequently brushed/raised in finishing to create a fuzzy pile.', 2, '{"french-terry"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Waffle knit (thermal knit) fabric''s distinctive 3D honeycomb/grid surface texture is created primarily through:', '["A special dye pattern applied after plain knitting","A patterned combination of tuck and knit stitches (similar in principle to pique but with a different, larger-scale repeat) that creates raised pockets/cells, which also trap air for added insulation — a functional as well as aesthetic structural effect","Weaving a grid pattern onto finished knit fabric","Only achievable through warp knitting, never weft knitting"]'::jsonb, 'A patterned combination of tuck and knit stitches (similar in principle to pique but with a different, larger-scale repeat) that creates raised pockets/cells, which also trap air for added insulation — a functional as well as aesthetic structural effect', 'Like pique, waffle/thermal knit structure uses patterned tuck stitches combined with regular knitting to create a genuinely three-dimensional cellular surface (rather than a flat printed pattern) — the raised pockets this creates trap air, giving waffle knit fabric a real functional insulation benefit beyond its distinctive textured appearance.', 3, '{"waffle-knit"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Ponte Roma'' (Ponte di Roma) knit fabric, popular for structured leggings and dresses, is a double-knit interlock-derivative structure valued mainly for its:', '["Extremely lightweight, sheer, see-through quality","Substantial body/stability with moderate stretch, good opacity, and a smooth, structured drape that holds garment shape well — closer to a stable, dense fabric than a loose, highly stretchy jersey","Complete lack of any stretch, behaving exactly like a rigid woven fabric","Being producible only in pure white with no dyeing possible"]'::jsonb, 'Substantial body/stability with moderate stretch, good opacity, and a smooth, structured drape that holds garment shape well — closer to a stable, dense fabric than a loose, highly stretchy jersey', 'Ponte Roma''s double-knit (interlock-family) construction gives it notably more body, stability, and opacity than single jersey, while retaining useful stretch — this combination of structure and moderate stretch is exactly why it''s favored for more tailored, structured knit garments like ponte leggings and fitted dresses, rather than loose, drapey jersey applications.', 3, '{"ponte-roma"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Birdseye knit structure (a small, repeating dot/diamond texture pattern, distinct from birdseye piqué in weaving) is created in knitting through:', '["A random, uncontrolled machine malfunction","A small-repeat combination of tuck stitches arranged in a specific pattern to create the characteristic tiny raised dot texture, commonly used in cloth diapers and some polo-style fabrics","Printing a dot pattern onto plain jersey after knitting","A structure that requires no tuck or float stitches at all"]'::jsonb, 'A small-repeat combination of tuck stitches arranged in a specific pattern to create the characteristic tiny raised dot texture, commonly used in cloth diapers and some polo-style fabrics', 'Like pique and waffle, birdseye knit texture is a genuine structural effect from patterned tuck-stitch placement (at a smaller repeat scale creating tiny dots rather than pique''s larger cells) — this structural, absorbent, textured fabric has traditionally been popular for cloth diapers and some knit polo-style textures.', 3, '{"birdseye-knit"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A knit fabric technologist observes ''oil stain'' defects (localized dark, greasy marks) appearing periodically at consistent intervals on fabric from one specific circular knitting machine. The most likely root cause, paralleling a similar issue in weaving, is:', '["The yarn''s dye lot, unrelated to the specific machine","Over-lubrication or a leaking oil point at a specific needle/cam/sinker station on that machine, dripping onto the fabric at a position that recurs with each cylinder revolution","The knitting machine''s RPM setting alone, with no relation to lubrication","A finishing department issue occurring after knitting, unrelated to the knitting stage"]'::jsonb, 'Over-lubrication or a leaking oil point at a specific needle/cam/sinker station on that machine, dripping onto the fabric at a position that recurs with each cylinder revolution', 'Similar to the woven fabric oil-stain troubleshooting principle, a defect recurring at a consistent interval tied to ONE specific machine points to a fixed mechanical source on that machine — an over-lubricated bearing, needle, or cam track dripping oil onto the fabric at the same relative position every cylinder rotation — rather than a raw material or downstream cause.', 3, '{"oil-stain","troubleshooting"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Pilling (small balls of tangled fiber forming on a fabric''s surface after wear/washing) tends to be a MORE significant quality concern for certain knit fabrics than others. Which fabric characteristic increases pilling risk most?', '["Using only long-staple, high-quality combed yarn with low hairiness","Using low-quality, short-staple, or high-hairiness yarn (where loose fiber ends protrude and tangle together under abrasion), especially in loosely structured or brushed/napped knit fabrics","Using only continuous filament synthetic yarn with zero staple fiber content","Pilling risk is identical for all yarn and fabric types regardless of fiber/yarn quality"]'::jsonb, 'Using low-quality, short-staple, or high-hairiness yarn (where loose fiber ends protrude and tangle together under abrasion), especially in loosely structured or brushed/napped knit fabrics', 'Pilling forms when loose, protruding fiber ends (more common with short-staple, low-quality, or hairy yarn) get tangled together through wear abrasion — this is why higher-quality combed yarn (lower hairiness) reduces pilling risk, and why loosely knitted or brushed/napped fabrics (with more free fiber ends available) tend to pill more than tightly knitted, smooth structures, paralleling the biopolishing concept from wet processing.', 2, '{"pilling"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A double-bed (V-bed) flat knitting machine can produce fully-reversible, double-faced fabric structures that a single-bed machine cannot. This capability comes from:', '["Having exactly the same single needle bed as any circular machine","Having two opposing needle beds set at an angle to each other, allowing needles from both beds to interact and form structures (like true rib, interlock-family fabrics, or fully-fashioned 3D shapes) impossible with only one bed of needles","Using no needles at all, only sinkers","Only being capable of knitting a single flat, single-faced fabric, identical to a single-bed machine"]'::jsonb, 'Having two opposing needle beds set at an angle to each other, allowing needles from both beds to interact and form structures (like true rib, interlock-family fabrics, or fully-fashioned 3D shapes) impossible with only one bed of needles', 'The defining feature of V-bed (double-bed) flat knitting is its two needle beds angled toward each other in a V-shape, allowing needles from each bed to transfer loops and interact — this is what enables true rib and interlock-type double-faced structures, plus advanced capabilities like stitch transfer for shaping and 3D knitted structures, none of which a single flat needle bed alone can achieve.', 3, '{"v-bed-flat-knitting"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Sinker ring'' or ''sinker cap'' assemblies on a circular knitting machine must be precisely synchronized with the needle cylinder''s rotation. A timing misalignment between the sinker action and needle movement would most likely cause:', '["No effect on fabric quality whatsoever","Loop formation faults (e.g., inconsistent loop size, dropped stitches, or press-offs) since the sinkers'' job of holding down old loops at the correct moment relative to needle rise/fall is disrupted, directly damaging the loop-forming process","An automatic improvement in fabric GSM consistency","A change in the yarn''s fiber content"]'::jsonb, 'Loop formation faults (e.g., inconsistent loop size, dropped stitches, or press-offs) since the sinkers'' job of holding down old loops at the correct moment relative to needle rise/fall is disrupted, directly damaging the loop-forming process', 'Loop formation is a tightly choreographed interaction between needle movement (cam-driven) and sinker movement (holding old loops at the precise right moment) — any timing misalignment between these two systems directly disrupts correct loop formation, showing up as inconsistent stitch size, dropped stitches, or more severe press-off faults, making sinker-needle timing a critical, regularly checked machine calibration parameter.', 3, '{"sinker-timing"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Auto-striper circular knitting machines (capable of automatically changing yarn color/feeder mid-production to create horizontal stripe patterns) achieve precise, repeatable stripe sequences through:', '["Manual yarn changing by an operator for every single stripe, with no automation","Programmable yarn-feeder selection devices that automatically switch which color yarn feeds at specific, pre-set course intervals, following a programmed stripe sequence without manual intervention","Dyeing the fabric in stripes after knitting is complete","Weaving a stripe pattern onto already-knitted fabric"]'::jsonb, 'Programmable yarn-feeder selection devices that automatically switch which color yarn feeds at specific, pre-set course intervals, following a programmed stripe sequence without manual intervention', 'Auto-striper machines use electronically or mechanically controlled yarn-changing devices at selected feeders, programmed to switch between multiple yarn colors at defined course counts — this automates what would otherwise require constant manual yarn changes, enabling efficient, consistent, repeatable stripe patterns at full production speed.', 2, '{"auto-striper"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Two knit fabrics have the same fiber content, yarn count, and GSM, but Fabric X uses single jersey structure and Fabric Y uses rib structure. Fabric Y will generally show:', '["Identical widthwise stretch% to Fabric X, since GSM and yarn count are the same","Meaningfully higher widthwise stretch% and better recovery than Fabric X, since structure (not just GSM/yarn count) is a major independent determinant of a knit fabric''s stretch behavior","Zero stretch in any direction, unlike single jersey","Lower GSM than Fabric X despite the stated equal GSM, which is a contradiction"]'::jsonb, 'Meaningfully higher widthwise stretch% and better recovery than Fabric X, since structure (not just GSM/yarn count) is a major independent determinant of a knit fabric''s stretch behavior', 'This question tests understanding that knit STRUCTURE, not just GSM or yarn count, is an independent major driver of fabric mechanical behavior — rib''s alternating knit/purl wale structure gives it substantially more inherent widthwise stretch and recovery than single jersey, even when GSM and yarn count are matched, which is exactly why rib is specifically chosen for cuffs/waistbands needing maximum stretch.', 3, '{"structure-vs-properties"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Relative humidity control in a knitting mill (similar to the concern in spinning) matters because:', '["Humidity has zero effect on knitting yarn behavior","Yarn (especially natural fiber) flexibility, static behavior, and friction against needles/sinkers/guides are humidity-sensitive, so poorly controlled humidity can increase yarn breakage, static-related processing issues, and even affect loop formation consistency","Only synthetic yarn is affected by humidity, never natural fiber, in knitting specifically","Humidity only matters in the dyeing stage, never during knitting itself"]'::jsonb, 'Yarn (especially natural fiber) flexibility, static behavior, and friction against needles/sinkers/guides are humidity-sensitive, so poorly controlled humidity can increase yarn breakage, static-related processing issues, and even affect loop formation consistency', 'Just as in spinning, cotton and other natural fiber yarns'' pliability and static behavior change with moisture content, directly affecting how smoothly yarn passes through knitting needles, sinkers, and guides — mills control humidity in knitting areas for the same underlying reason as in spinning: too-dry conditions increase static and breakage risk, undermining consistent, high-quality loop formation.', 2, '{"humidity-control","knitting"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A production planner calculating expected fabric output (kg/hour) for a circular knitting machine needs which combination of factors, at minimum?', '["Only the machine''s cylinder diameter","Machine RPM, number of feeders, loop length (yarn consumption per course), and machine efficiency%, since all of these together determine how much yarn mass is actually knitted into fabric per unit time","Only the yarn''s fiber content, unrelated to machine settings","Only the fabric''s target color"]'::jsonb, 'Machine RPM, number of feeders, loop length (yarn consumption per course), and machine efficiency%, since all of these together determine how much yarn mass is actually knitted into fabric per unit time', 'Knit fabric production rate calculation mirrors the same principle as loom output calculation: it requires the machine''s cycle rate (RPM), how many courses form per cycle (feeder count), how much yarn mass each course actually consumes (a function of loop length and yarn count), and the real-world efficiency% (accounting for stoppages) — omitting any of these gives an incomplete or inaccurate production estimate.', 3, '{"production-calc","knitting"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A knit fabric mill is asked to switch a plain single jersey style to an identical-looking style but now requiring both ''good stretch'' AND ''minimal curling at cut edges'' for easier garment sewing. What is the most appropriate structural recommendation?', '["Keep single jersey exactly as-is, since curling has no practical sewing impact","Recommend switching to (or blending in) a rib or interlock-family structure, since these structures inherently resist the edge-curling that single jersey shows while still providing good stretch, directly addressing the stated sewing concern","Switch to a woven fabric instead, eliminating stretch entirely","Increase single jersey''s loop length only, which has no effect on curling"]'::jsonb, 'Recommend switching to (or blending in) a rib or interlock-family structure, since these structures inherently resist the edge-curling that single jersey shows while still providing good stretch, directly addressing the stated sewing concern', 'This is a direct, practical application of the earlier curling/rib principles: since single jersey''s asymmetric loop structure is the root cause of its curling tendency, and rib/interlock structures balance this asymmetry while retaining (or even improving) stretch, recommending a structural change (not just a finishing fix) is the technically correct response to a stated ''stretch AND non-curling'' requirement.', 3, '{"structure-selection","sewing"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'intermediate', 'multiple_choice', 'Micro-mesh knit fabric (common in sportswear for ventilation) achieves its open, ventilated structure primarily through:', '["Using extremely thick yarn at very high stitch density","A deliberately open knit structure — often combining tuck/miss stitches or specific warp-knit patterning — that leaves visible small holes/openings in the fabric for airflow while maintaining structural integrity","Cutting small holes into finished, solid jersey fabric after knitting","A structure with zero engineered openness, relying only on thin yarn"]'::jsonb, 'A deliberately open knit structure — often combining tuck/miss stitches or specific warp-knit patterning — that leaves visible small holes/openings in the fabric for airflow while maintaining structural integrity', 'Mesh fabrics are engineered open structures (via specific stitch combinations, often float/miss stitch patterns in weft knit or specialized lapping patterns in warp knit) that create controlled, structurally stable openings for airflow — a designed structural feature, not a post-production cutting or perforation process, which would compromise fabric integrity and unravel at the cut edges.', 2, '{"micro-mesh"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A knit technologist is asked to explain why two garments, both labeled ''180 GSM 100% cotton single jersey,'' feel noticeably different in stretch and drape. Assuming the GSM and fiber content claims are accurate, the most likely explanation is:', '["GSM and fiber content alone fully guarantee identical stretch and drape, so this scenario is impossible","Different combinations of yarn count and loop length can achieve the same GSM while producing different stitch density, and machine gauge, knitting tension, and finishing (e.g., compacting degree) can further differentiate hand-feel and stretch even at matched GSM and fiber content","The two garments must have been mislabeled, since GSM alone always determines feel","Only dye color could explain any feel difference between the two"]'::jsonb, 'Different combinations of yarn count and loop length can achieve the same GSM while producing different stitch density, and machine gauge, knitting tension, and finishing (e.g., compacting degree) can further differentiate hand-feel and stretch even at matched GSM and fiber content', 'GSM is a single aggregate number that can be reached through many different combinations of yarn count, loop length, and gauge — two fabrics with identical GSM and fiber content can still have very different stitch density, structure tightness, and finishing treatment, all of which meaningfully affect stretch and drape, illustrating why GSM alone is an incomplete fabric specification.', 3, '{"GSM-limitations","fabric-spec"}'::text[]);
